From PEPSWIN Google Group

---------- Forwarded message ---------
From: clasy...@gmail.com <Unknown>
Date: Friday, October 18, 2019 at 10:31:27 AM UTC-5
Subject: Newest PEPS Command Window (for use with all Windows releases 
prior to Windows 10)
To: pepswin <Unknown>


A decent amount of update and cleanup as I can finally see a potential 
release, time permitting.  A lot of stuff was a bit sloppy and not quite 
"clean" enough, and of course the entire Windows 10 debacle which has a 
less than satisfying solution, but after a bumpy ride so to speak, it can 
be used in various alternative ways, some better than others.  I have to do 
some work to show all of the attempts, and I will post what all of them 
look like likely fairly soon, now that we are certain of what Microsoft has 
and has not done, etc.  It's a waste of time, but I do want to have some 
support for Windows 10, etc.

In the meantime take a look at the new command window, which includes some 
new features.  I have to do a major update on the documentation before it 
matches the program, etc.

There is an issue with the icons:

This is a closer set to what I would like, and I am still looking for some 
volunteers to design a few of them.  I can easily make the P?S/8-oriented 
and P?S/8 FOCAL-oriented examples conform to the others and just maintain 
the usual color scheme clues, but I really want a set of functional icons 
where the typical P?S/8 question mark design is its proper symbol.  Thus, I 
am actively seeking both feedback and aid regarding this.  Likely the 
release will make the P?S/8 icons "conform" as stated above for at least 
current parity with the situation as it is, but it's not what I want, but I 
am at the upper end of my meager "artistic" abilities.  Some of these were 
made with Junior Icon Editor, etc. In that utility, it's not that hard to 
make color changes for these simplistic icons, but I am out of my league 
(and pay-grade!)

In any case, the paper-tape stuff is behind "other systems" as well as the 
older P?S/8 Version 8T icons retained to allow comparison with that ancient 
system.  It's not on the desktop to avoid clutter and is only of interest 
to a few people.  I am still disappointed with SIMH as the paper-tape stuff 
is incomplete and goes contrary to the built-in documentation.  But the 
primary purpose of even having the paper-tape stuff is to illustrate just 
how frustrating it is to use a PDP-8 without a storage device.  Paper-tape 
really sucks, but if we are to educate new people, they have to suffer a 
bit as well, so they can understand this. Running SIMH "on the metal" means 
having to load into memory a whole bunch of paper-tapes in a repetitive 
sequence, and worse still, a lot of tape action using them, and in the case 
of some of them, setting front panel switches to make them do what little 
they can do.  But it does get the "flavor" of just how dismal the dark old 
days before DECtape, etc. were.

If they manage to upgrade the paper-tape stuff to be complete, I would 
consider having a paper-tape-only icon on the desktop, but considering it 
is not even all there, for now relegating it to the "other systems" 
position along with other things few will care about.  I am disappointed 
because I wanted to document it being complete and "useful" if only to 
beginners who cannot gauge real-time considerations, and then let them be 
blown away by the choices that boot off of a device, etc.  There will be 
other alternatives on that top line (or lines) as other configurations 
become viable/stable.  Currently, P?S/8 Version 8Z for RX01 is available, 
and I actually developed it using PEPS, etc.  But it's a poor choice for an 
emulator because of the limited support of disk space; this is a hardware 
limitation of the RX01, and is not a really useful configuration for any 
operating system, etc.  But there are real-world machines  using the RX01 
that are now using P?S/8 in fact.  Look for more powerful emulated media 
later, most especially the RK8E/RK05.

Ask away if any of the functions in the Command Window are not clear.  
Documentation is understandably behind, so for now, questions are welcome.

cjl

